Now I understand the vulnerability. Let me analyze `lex_multiline_string`:

```c
static void *lex_multiline_string(struct lexer *lexer)
{
    char *end = (peek(lexer) == '"') ? "\"\"\"" : "'''";

    advance_n(lexer, strlen("'''") - 1);  // advance 2 chars

    do {
        if (!strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)) {
            emit(lexer, LEXEME_STRING);
            lexer->pos += 3;
            return lex_config;
        }
    } while (next(lexer) != '\0');

    return lex_error(lexer, "EOF while scanning multiline string");
}
```

The bug: `strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)` reads 3 bytes from `lexer->pos`. If the input ends right after the opening `'''` or `"""` (i.e., `lexer->pos` points near the end of the buffer), `strncmp` reads past the end of the buffer — a global-buffer-overflow READ of 3 bytes.

The fuzzer copies data into a static 32768-byte buffer. So the input is placed in a static global buffer. If the input is exactly `'''` (3 bytes) or `"""`, then after `advance_n(lexer, 2)`, `lexer->pos` points to the byte after the last quote (which is the end of the copied data, but within the static buffer). Then `strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)` reads 3 bytes from `lexer->pos`. If `lexer->pos` is at the very end of the static buffer (32768 bytes), it reads past the global buffer.

Wait, but the fuzzer copies `size` bytes into `static_data`. The static buffer is 32768 bytes. If we provide exactly 32768 bytes of input, then `lexer->pos` could reach the end of the static buffer.

Let me think about the exact trigger. The input needs to start with `'''` or `"""` to enter `lex_multiline_string`. Then `advance_n(lexer, 2)` moves pos forward by 2. Then in the loop, `strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)` reads 3 bytes.

For the read to go past the end of the static buffer, `lexer->pos` must be at `static_data + 32768` or beyond. 

